{0 Conventions: how the bindings are organised}

{b OUTLINE — to be written by hand.} Each section lists what to cover and
suggested points. The diagrams are drafts to keep, change or drop. Remove
this paragraph and the [TODO] notes when the page is written.

{1 Who this page is for}

TODO: one paragraph. Readers who know GTK from C (or another binding) and
want to know where things are in OCaml, and readers new to GTK who need the
mental model before the per-class pages make sense.

{1 The two layers}

TODO: cover
- Layer 1 (the [Wrappers] modules): one module per class, an abstract
  handle type [t], one function per C function. Closest to the C API.
- Layer 2 (the class modules): one module per class, holding an OCaml
  class type, a class, and constructors. Methods, signals and properties
  are object methods. This is the layer most code should use.
- Both layers wrap the same objects; you can move between them (see
  {{!section-converting}Converting between the layers}).
- When to use Layer 1: interop, functions with no Layer 2 form, or
  avoiding objects in performance-sensitive code (check that this claim
  holds before writing it).

{@mermaid[
flowchart TD
  C["C library (libgtk-4, libgio-2.0, ...)"]
  L1["Layer 1: Gtk.Wrappers.Button
  type t, one function per C function"]
  L2["Layer 2: Gtk.Button
  class type button_t, class button, new_* constructors"]
  APP["Your code"]
  C --> L1 --> L2
  APP -- "usually" --> L2
  APP -. "interop, low level" .-> L1
]}

{1 Libraries and namespaces}

TODO: cover
- One library per GObject namespace ([Ocgtk_gtk], [Ocgtk_gio], ...), with
  the [Ocgtk] umbrella module re-exporting them; link the
  {{!page-index}package index}.
- Each library has a namespace module named after the C namespace
  ([Gtk], [Gio], [GdkPixbuf], ...). Start there: it lists every class,
  interface, enumeration and constant.
- Typical [open]: [open Ocgtk_gtk.Gtk] (match the examples directory).

{1:anatomy Anatomy of a class module}

TODO: walk through one class end to end (suggest [Gtk.Button]: small, has
several constructors, a signal, an interface).
- [class type button_t]: the object's methods, signals and properties.
  Grouped on its page into Signals, Methods, Properties and Conversions.
- [class button]: wraps an existing Layer 1 handle. Not normally called
  directly; use the constructors.
- [new_], [new_with_label], ...: the constructors. They return
  [button_t].
- Why the class type is [button_t] and not [t]: types in signatures and
  error messages read as [Button.button_t], not an anonymous [t].

{@mermaid[
flowchart LR
  subgraph NS["Ocgtk_gtk.Gtk (namespace module)"]
    direction TB
    subgraph L2M["Button (Layer 2)"]
      CT["class type button_t
      methods, signals, properties"]
      CL["class button : Wrappers.Button.t -> button_t"]
      CTOR["new_ / new_with_label / ...
      return button_t"]
    end
    subgraph W["Wrappers"]
      L1M["Button (Layer 1)
      type t, new_, get_label, set_label, ..."]
    end
    ENUMS["Enumerations: polymorphic variants"]
    CONSTS["Constants"]
  end
  CTOR --> CL --> CT
  CL -. wraps .-> L1M
]}

{1 Creating objects}

TODO: cover the four cases each class page states in its canned text.
- The class has constructors: call one ([Button.new_with_label "OK"]).
- Abstract class: no constructor; create a subclass instead (for example
  [Widget]).
- Interface: no constructor; values come from methods or functions, or
  from objects that implement it.
- Neither: the object is returned by methods on other classes (for example
  a [Display], a [Clipboard], an event).

{@mermaid[
flowchart TD
  Q{"Does the class page have a
  Constructors section?"}
  Q -- yes --> A["Call a constructor:
  Button.new_with_label"]
  Q -- no --> K{"What does the page say?"}
  K -- "abstract class" --> S["Create a subclass,
  e.g. Button for Widget"]
  K -- "interface" --> I["Use an object that implements it,
  or a function that returns it"]
  K -- "returned by other classes" --> R["Get it from a method,
  e.g. a display or a clipboard"]
]}

{1 Inheritance and interfaces}

TODO: cover
- How the C hierarchy maps onto class types: a class type [inherit]s its
  parent's class type and the class types of the interfaces it
  implements. Each class type page lists "Inherits from" and
  "Implements".
- Upcasting with [:>] (for example [(button :> Widget.widget_t)]).
- Downcasting / runtime type checks: say what is and is not supported
  (check the current API before writing).
- [as_*] methods return the Layer 1 handle.
- Cyclic groups: some modules have long generated names such as
  [GEvent_controller_and__..._widget]. These are internal; use the public
  module ([Gtk.Widget]).

{@mermaid[
classDiagram
  direction BT
  class widget_t {
    show()
    ...()
  }
  class actionable_t {
    <<interface>>
    get_action_name()
  }
  class button_t {
    on_clicked()
    get_label()
    set_label()
    as_button()
  }
  button_t --|> widget_t : inherit
  button_t ..|> actionable_t : inherit
]}

{1 Methods and naming}

TODO: a mapping table from C to OCaml. Suggested rows:
- [gtk_button_set_label (b, "x")] → Layer 2 [b#set_label "x"],
  Layer 1 [Wrappers.Button.set_label b "x"].
- [GtkButton] → [Gtk.Button] (module), [button_t] (class type).
- Constructor [gtk_button_new_with_label] → [Button.new_with_label].
- Constants and enumeration members → [`UPPER_CASE] polymorphic variants
  (enumerations) and flag lists (bitfields); say which.
- Reserved words and other renamings ([new_], [type_], ...).
- Each generated doc names the C function it wraps, with a link to the
  upstream C documentation.

{1 Types}

TODO: one short rule per row, with an example each:
- Nullable values → [option].
- [GError**] → [result] with {!GError.t}.
- Out parameters → tuples (check how multiple outputs are returned).
- Arrays and lists → OCaml arrays or lists (say which, and when).
- Strings: UTF-8 OCaml [string].
- Sized integers → the modules in [Ocgtk_common] ([Int32], [UInt8], ...).
- Records and boxed types: what you can access (field accessors).

{1 Signals}

TODO: cover
- Each signal is an [on_<name>] method: [?after:bool], a labelled
  [~callback], and [()]; it returns a handler id.
- Callback arguments are labelled ([~x ~y ~width ~height]).
- Disconnecting and blocking with the handler id (see {!Gobject}).
- Signals that return a value (for example to stop propagation).

{@mermaid[
sequenceDiagram
  participant App as Your code
  participant B as button_t
  participant G as GObject signal system
  App->>B: b#35;on_clicked ~callback ()
  B->>G: connect "clicked"
  G-->>App: handler id
  Note over G: user clicks the button
  G->>App: callback ()
]}

